        "- **Demand in smaller cities.** Entering 15–20 new cities means demand for licensed drivers and fleet partners where the platform has not operated so far. Urban points out that licensing times differ: in Warsaw about four months for a taxi licence and over a year for a driver ID, in Kraków and the Tricity two to three working days.\n- **Stronger competition for drivers.** Aiming to double rides in a market growing 5–10% a year points to a fight with Uber and Bolt for passengers and drivers, including promotions.\n- **North American tourists as new customers.** Roaming in Warsaw may add orders from the airport and hotels; the company has not said how many customers it expects.\n- **App migration in 2027.** For fleets this may mean new tools (driver app, partner panel, settlement reports). No details yet.\n- **Regulation.** Freenow opposes including taxis in the future Polish rules implementing Directive (EU) 2024/2831 and points to risks in the proposed presumption of employment and joint liability of platform and fleet partner. This is the company's position on draft rules, not law in force ([see: Poland will miss the 2 December deadline](/en/article/platform-work-directive-poland-will-miss-deadline)). It supports stricter verification of non-EU drivers, including criminal-record certificates from the country of origin.",
